course: dictionary
lesson: 3016
title: Dye: one word, two jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Dye can be a noun or a verb. As a noun, a dye is the substance that changes colour. «Pysanky are Ukrainian Easter eggs decorated with wax and dye.» Here dye is a thing that is used, so it is a noun.
explain: As a verb, to dye means to change something's colour by soaking it in dye or covering it with dye. The words around it are the clues. «People dye their hair» tells you that someone does something to hair, so dye is an action word here.
explain: Dye sounds the same as die, so context must settle which one is meant. A dye for cloth makes sense in a sentence about colour, but die would not. Check by swapping in your meaning and reading the sentence again.
fact: A dye is a substance used to change the colour of cloth, hair, food or other things.
fact: Dyes can be made from plants, insects or chemicals.
fact: «The red food colour carmine is made from crushed cochineal bugs that live on cactus», and the Aztecs used it to dye cloth.
fact: In history, purple dye was once so rare and costly that only kings and emperors wore it.
fact: «Dyes can also leave: they wash out, rub off and fade in sunlight.»
exercise: «Pysanky are Ukrainian Easter eggs decorated with wax and dye.» In this sentence, what kind of word is dye? | A describing word, an adjective | An action word, a verb | *A thing, a noun | A joining word, a conjunction
exercise: «In science, food dye in water shows how water moves up a celery stalk.» What does dye mean here? | *A substance that changes colour | To stop water moving | A type of celery | To soak celery in water
exercise: «Dyes can also leave: they wash out, rub off and fade in sunlight.» What do the clues tell you about dyes? | They can only be eaten | *They can come off or fade over time | They are always made from insects | They always stay bright for ever
test: «She decided to dye her old T-shirt blue.» What does dye mean here? | A coloured powder sold in a tin | To wash something clean | To throw something away | *To change the colour of something by soaking it
test: «Litmus paper turns red in acid and blue in alkali; its dye comes from lichens.» Where does this dye come from? | Cochineal bugs | *Lichens | Celery stalks | Ukrainian farms
exam: «Dyes can also leave: they wash out, rub off and fade in sunlight.» What does leave mean in this sentence? | Go away on holiday | Stay in one place for ever | Pay for something in a shop | *Come off or fade away
